---
title: Use the bundled libraries
description: Start an app with the optional SDK libraries without building the framework.
sidebar:
  order: 6
---

The preview.2 SDK includes eight optional Luau modules. They are **source
libraries**, not extra global objects: your project must declare their files
before `require("@Library/Module")` can load them. They do not download packages
or start background services just because you import them.

## Choose a starting project

Run these commands from your extracted SDK folder. The `../` destinations are
new application folders beside it. Open that application folder in VS Code and
choose the same SDK.

| I need | Start with |
| --- | --- |
| Named actions and shared button/shortcut commands | `./lui.cmd new ../MyCommands --template commands` |
| Commands, documents, pane state, divider dragging or preferences | `./lui.cmd new ../MyWorkbench --template workbench` |
| JSON encoding/decoding or an HTTP JSON client | Copy the complete `Examples/json-client` project. |
| A number field with step buttons | Copy the complete `Examples/numeric-input` project. |

For example, from the SDK folder:

```powershell
Copy-Item -LiteralPath ./Examples/numeric-input -Destination ../MyNumberApp -Recurse
./lui.cmd check ../MyNumberApp/lui.json
./lui.cmd run ../MyNumberApp/lui.json
```

**Expected result:** the copied app shows the numeric input example. Edit the
copy, not the SDK’s original files; SDK integrity checks expect those originals
to remain unchanged. Save files and use **LUI: Restart App** after edits.

Keep `Vendor/`, `lui.json`, `Modules.lock.json` and `LuiToolchain.json` with the
project. Copying only a Main.luau import into a basic starter leaves out required
dependencies. The module lock records the declared vendor bytes; the toolchain
pin selects the SDK. They solve different problems.

## What each module does

| Module | Use it for | What it does not do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Commands](../../libraries/luiworkbench/commands/) | Register actions and invoke them from buttons, palettes or shortcuts. | Draw a palette/menu automatically or capture global keyboard input. |
| [Documents](../../libraries/luiworkbench/documents/) | Track text buffers, dirty state and load/save results. | Open arbitrary disk paths or perform the actual file write. |
| [Workspace](../../libraries/luiworkbench/workspace/) | Track document IDs in two panes and calculate layout widths. | Create floating windows or arbitrary docking trees. |
| [Split](../../libraries/luiworkbench/split/) | Track a primary-mouse divider gesture. | Own the UI or introduce a second input system. |
| [Preferences](../../libraries/luiworkbench/preferences/) | Store small ordinary settings using scoped StorageService. | Encrypt secrets or provide transactions across keys. |
| [Json](../../libraries/json/json/) | Encode/decode bounded JSON, including explicit null and empty containers. | Perform HTTP or validate your app’s data schema. |
| [JsonClient](../../libraries/jsonclient/jsonclient/) | Make an HTTP request and decode its JSON body. | Treat malformed JSON as a successful typed response. |
| [NumericInput](../../libraries/luiui/numericinput/) | Provide a bounded number field with step buttons. | Add a new core Instance class or a locale-aware number parser. |

Each module page has a complete example, setup, expected result, limits and
expandable exported types. A model/controller is an ordinary Luau object returned
by the module; it is not automatically part of the Instance tree.

## Save ordinary preferences

The workbench starter already declares storage. For a different preview.2 app,
add `Storage` to its existing Capabilities array and add this top-level field:

```json
"Storage": { "ApplicationId": "com.example.notes" }
```

That is a **field fragment**, not a complete lui.json. Keep the rest of your
manifest. The supplied desktop runner provisions the app-private provider;
ordinary SDK app authors do not call native installation functions.

Use the same project folder when testing persistence. Restarting it preserves
data; a copied or packaged project has separate data. Changing ApplicationId
also selects a different scope. This storage is unencrypted and is not a safe
place for passwords or access tokens.

## App code versus private support files

Use the eight public imports listed above. Some complete examples also contain
support modules such as Scope, State or VirtualList. They help those examples
manage cleanup/rendering and may be required by a public component’s content
lock. Keep required support files with that component, but do not assume every
vendored helper is a separately supported library API.

The library catalogs still use the label Development to describe experimental
API maturity. These eight modules are nevertheless included in the released
preview.2 SDK. Python repository-staging commands in older source notes are for
framework contributors; you can start from the bundled projects above.
